00:00:14.090 --> 00:00:15.210
It's really great to have you on.

00:00:15.210 --> 00:00:16.210
I appreciate it.

00:00:16.210 --> 00:00:17.210
Hey, David.

00:00:17.210 --> 00:00:18.280
Thanks for having me.

00:00:18.280 --> 00:00:24.850
So to start with denialism, how far back does
denialism go as far as we know?

00:00:24.850 --> 00:00:33.000
I mean, were there early denial lists when
there were the original theories about what

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:40.469
are humans and what is outer space and is
whether God or simply the machinations of

00:00:40.469 --> 00:00:44.480
climate or I mean, like how far back does
denialism go in some way, shape or form?

00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:45.480
Well, well, yes.

00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:46.800
All of the all of the above.

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:51.540
I would I would send it back several million
years, as a matter of fact.

00:00:51.540 --> 00:00:59.100
So so Homo sapiens evolved in small groups
and going back further to our ancestors.

00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:01.219
They were in the same situation.

00:01:01.219 --> 00:01:02.219
And.

00:01:02.219 --> 00:01:04.999
The it's a really important adaptive trait.

00:01:04.999 --> 00:01:09.090
If you're living in a small room to be able
to integrate yourself into the group's ideological

00:01:09.090 --> 00:01:15.369
worldview, you know, if your group worships
Poseidon, you better figure out a way to believe

00:01:15.369 --> 00:01:16.409
in Poseidon.

00:01:16.409 --> 00:01:18.259
Otherwise, you're out.

00:01:18.259 --> 00:01:19.820
For example.

00:01:19.820 --> 00:01:21.189
Right.

00:01:21.189 --> 00:01:27.259
So it's really deep, deep in our evolutionary
history that we have formed this talent for

00:01:27.259 --> 00:01:33.759
rationalizing beliefs and our beliefs in order
to integrate with a with a larger worldview.

00:01:33.759 --> 00:01:35.319
So, yes.

00:01:35.319 --> 00:01:39.849
And so then that moves forward through history,
all kinds of religious beliefs, obviously.

00:01:39.849 --> 00:01:44.329
That's probably the beginning of it all.

00:01:44.329 --> 00:01:46.029
Beliefs about other groups.

00:01:46.029 --> 00:01:49.619
Because what are our evolutionary context
was we evolved in small groups that were in

00:01:49.619 --> 00:01:51.759
competition with other groups.

00:01:51.759 --> 00:01:55.869
So if we hate the group, if you know, if our
group hates the group across the river, I'm

00:01:55.869 --> 00:01:59.549
going to find reasons to hate that group,
too.

00:01:59.549 --> 00:02:03.979
Otherwise, I'm not able to integrate myself
into my own group because we don't we don't

00:02:03.979 --> 00:02:07.149
survive well on our own cooperation, social
cooperation.

00:02:07.149 --> 00:02:08.629
That's our evolutionary niche.

00:02:08.629 --> 00:02:11.200
That's that's that's what gives us our advantage.

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:12.540
That's interesting.

00:02:12.540 --> 00:02:19.900
So early on, there was an evolutionarily advantageous
reason why we were able to sort of potentially

00:02:19.900 --> 00:02:26.030
suppress contradictory information in order
to be in line with with our group.

00:02:26.030 --> 00:02:29.060
Does that still hold true today in the same
way?

00:02:29.060 --> 00:02:32.959
I mean, is it an issue of evolutionary advantage
at this point?

00:02:32.959 --> 00:02:37.609
Well, it's well, a lot of things that in the
past were an advantage aren't necessarily

00:02:37.609 --> 00:02:39.659
an advantage now, but it's still there.

00:02:39.659 --> 00:02:43.629
It's real deep, so deep in our cognitive makeup
that we we have these skills.

00:02:43.629 --> 00:02:47.780
We're actually very, very good at adapting
our beliefs, not just our behavior, but our

00:02:47.780 --> 00:02:50.730
beliefs to our situation.

00:02:50.730 --> 00:02:57.090
And you mentioned, you know, ignoring evidence
or picking out the evidence that you want,

00:02:57.090 --> 00:02:59.680
rationalizing what's called confirmation bias.

00:02:59.680 --> 00:03:00.680
Right.

00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:05.099
You ignore the evidence you don't want and
you seek out evidence or reasons to believe

00:03:05.099 --> 00:03:07.620
what you want to believe anyway.

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:12.769
And that becomes actually particularly toxic
in our current media environment, where you

00:03:12.769 --> 00:03:20.170
can go wherever you want to to get the world
view that that you want to believe.

00:03:20.170 --> 00:03:29.420
And you get the picture of the world presented
to you in in, you know, HD video and describe

00:03:29.420 --> 00:03:31.700
to you the way you want to hear it.

00:03:31.700 --> 00:03:33.030
And it you know.

00:03:33.030 --> 00:03:34.030
And so we have.

00:03:34.030 --> 00:03:37.819
But you know what's called these, you know,
media bubbles that people get into in addition

00:03:37.819 --> 00:03:42.100
to things like our geographic sorting here
in the United States in particular, peoples

00:03:42.100 --> 00:03:47.300
have sorted themselves very well geographically
into like minded communities.

00:03:47.300 --> 00:03:56.150
Is there any demographic trends that you see
in terms of denialism and and sort of rejection

00:03:56.150 --> 00:04:00.189
of disconfirms confirmatory evidence, maybe
like in terms of age?

00:04:00.189 --> 00:04:03.920
Is there a certain age at which this becomes
