00:04:11.409 --> 00:04:17.370
Well, you'll you'll hear different theories
from different social scientists about this.

00:04:17.370 --> 00:04:23.740
Younger people have demonstrated a little
more cognitive flexibility and openness.

00:04:23.740 --> 00:04:28.110
Older folks are, as you would say, you know,
are more set in their ways.

00:04:28.110 --> 00:04:30.710
They're also a little more sensitive to threat.

00:04:30.710 --> 00:04:32.030
They feel more threatened.

00:04:32.030 --> 00:04:35.000
And that's a really big factor here.

00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:40.520
If we also look at the progressive slash liberal
versus conservative ways of thinking about

00:04:40.520 --> 00:04:42.500
the world.

00:04:42.500 --> 00:04:49.780
So where conservatives and conservatives obviously
skew at this point in the US wider and older,

00:04:49.780 --> 00:04:56.030
obviously conservatives by definition are
what we call system justifier.

00:04:56.030 --> 00:04:58.889
They are pro status quo.

00:04:58.889 --> 00:05:02.840
Conservatives are comfortable with the way
things are in terms of the social and economic

00:05:02.840 --> 00:05:06.379
order and its hierarchies.

00:05:06.379 --> 00:05:13.639
And they will they will seek out reasons to
dismiss evidence that there's something wrong

00:05:13.639 --> 00:05:23.030
with that order, such as, you know, systemic
inequality and and younger people are less

00:05:23.030 --> 00:05:25.530
prone to do that.

00:05:25.530 --> 00:05:32.310
So it seems like there's an asymmetry there
in the sense that when you talk about the

00:05:32.310 --> 00:05:39.129
status quo and you think about inertia, certainly
it takes less energy to maintain the status

00:05:39.129 --> 00:05:40.129
quo in general.

00:05:40.129 --> 00:05:44.599
All else being equal than it would to change
the status quo.

00:05:44.599 --> 00:05:50.590
And so there is a sort of it seems that there
would be a structural advantage to those who

00:05:50.590 --> 00:05:57.099
are predisposed to keep things the same, because
it's easier to do so in a sense.

00:05:57.099 --> 00:06:01.340
Well, the status quo is upheld by all kinds
of systems.

00:06:01.340 --> 00:06:06.909
For example, the police and the police are
the in any society, the police are the tip

00:06:06.909 --> 00:06:11.580
of the spear when it comes to maintaining
the social and economic hierarchy.

00:06:11.580 --> 00:06:13.650
That's what they're.

00:06:13.650 --> 00:06:15.590
That's when we say law and order.

00:06:15.590 --> 00:06:18.310
That's what the order part of that means.

00:06:18.310 --> 00:06:21.789
You know, when Trump calls himself city law
and Nixon calls himself the law and Order,

00:06:21.789 --> 00:06:24.930
President Trump calls himself the law and
order president.

00:06:24.930 --> 00:06:26.419
That's what the order is.

00:06:26.419 --> 00:06:30.569
The order is maintaining the kurk current
social and economic hierarchy.

00:06:30.569 --> 00:06:41.039
And that includes, obviously, systems of systemic
racial bias and and systemic in structural

00:06:41.039 --> 00:06:43.099
economic inequality.

00:06:43.099 --> 00:06:51.909
I'm interested in climate change in this context
because we could think about a way in which

00:06:51.909 --> 00:06:59.590
denying climate change goes against the known
facts and may be advantageous if you're part

00:06:59.590 --> 00:07:01.189
of certain political groups.

00:07:01.189 --> 00:07:02.189
Right.

00:07:02.189 --> 00:07:07.020
So in order to keep the peace, as you suggest,
we may have this tendency to say, hey, let's

00:07:07.020 --> 00:07:10.789
I'm going to even if there is information
that suggests climate change is real and it's

00:07:10.789 --> 00:07:14.050
manmade, I'm going to stick with my group.

00:07:14.050 --> 00:07:20.520
But over the long term, if it's true what
science says about the climate, it's self-destructive

00:07:20.520 --> 00:07:22.530
to the human race and to the planet.

00:07:22.530 --> 00:07:29.490
So it seems that there is a long term dis
advantage when it comes to the sort of evolutionary

00:07:29.490 --> 00:07:30.490
aspect of it.

00:07:30.490 --> 00:07:34.199
How could how should we interpret climate
change, that denial in this context?

00:07:34.199 --> 00:07:37.010
David, I'm very interested in climate change
denial.

00:07:37.010 --> 00:07:39.320
So I'm glad you bring it out.

00:07:39.320 --> 00:07:44.230
The first thing I'd want to stress is that
being a climate science denier list has nothing

00:07:44.230 --> 00:07:48.610
to do with being stupid or being uneducated.

00:07:48.610 --> 00:07:51.330
In fact, what we see is the reverse.

00:07:51.330 --> 00:07:56.050
If you're if you're a conservative, you're
more likely to be a climate science denier.

00:07:56.050 --> 00:07:58.669
If you have a college education.

00:07:58.669 --> 00:08:03.180
You're more likely when you give people tests
not just on science literacy, but climate

00:08:03.180 --> 00:08:08.439
science literacy, you're more likely to be
a denier if you're conservative.

00:08:08.439 --> 00:08:12.320
When you score higher on climate science literacy
tests.

00:08:12.320 --> 00:08:15.190
So what does that telling us that telling
us is that we.

00:08:15.190 --> 00:08:17.590
We are really good at rationalizing our own
beliefs.

00:08:17.590 --> 00:08:22.550
The more information we have, the more ammunition
we get to rationalize those beliefs.

00:08:22.550 --> 00:08:26.139
This puts us in a classic game, theoretical
situation.

00:08:26.139 --> 00:08:31.780
I don't know if I want to get too technical
on this, but yeah, where individual decisions

00:08:31.780 --> 00:08:35.980
about what's best for them in terms of what
belief systems they want to hold on to become

00:08:35.980 --> 00:08:39.760
collectively absolutely disastrous in the
long run.

00:08:39.760 --> 00:08:41.140
So that's interesting.

00:08:41.140 --> 00:08:42.140
So.

00:08:42.140 --> 00:08:50.140
So in other words, the the more information
we have, maybe not the more that our beliefs

00:08:50.140 --> 00:08:53.160
align with what we would call an empirical
reality.

00:08:53.160 --> 00:08:57.700
But the better we are at justifying the beliefs
that we have.

00:08:57.700 --> 00:09:01.070
Well, we have more capacity to rationalize.

00:09:01.070 --> 00:09:05.360
It doesn't mean we have to, but there is definitely
a tendency to do so.

00:09:05.360 --> 00:09:10.010
I mean, we love to think of ourselves as these
purely rational beings, and all our beliefs

00:09:10.010 --> 00:09:14.580
are based on evidence and listening to qualified
experts.

00:09:14.580 --> 00:09:19.450
But in practice, that's not what happens when
there when it's beliefs, when when we're presented

00:09:19.450 --> 00:09:23.060
with facts that are threatening to us.

00:09:23.060 --> 00:09:29.650
So our our our very sense of identity is based
on the groups that we feel associated with

00:09:29.650 --> 00:09:33.290
our.

00:09:33.290 --> 00:09:35.960
Like I said, if I said, David, David, tell
me about yourself.

00:09:35.960 --> 00:09:36.960
Right.

00:09:36.960 --> 00:09:38.520
And I'm going to I'm going to make up some
things about you.

00:09:38.520 --> 00:09:40.410
So I say, hey, David, tell me about yourself.

00:09:40.410 --> 00:09:41.790
Who are you?

00:09:41.790 --> 00:09:44.920
And you'd say, well, like, well, you know,
I was I was born in Rhode Island, but I grew

00:09:44.920 --> 00:09:46.030
up in Texas.

00:09:46.030 --> 00:09:48.960
My parents immigrated from Ireland.

00:09:48.960 --> 00:09:50.270
I'm a Methodist.

00:09:50.270 --> 00:09:53.120
I'm a social conservative.

00:09:53.120 --> 00:09:57.300
But I feel strongly about the environment
and all of those facts that when you tell

00:09:57.300 --> 00:10:00.510
me all about yourself, all of those things
are are relations.

00:10:00.510 --> 00:10:02.790
You have two other groups.

00:10:02.790 --> 00:10:07.990
And in terms of their ideological worldview,
that's your identity.

00:10:07.990 --> 00:10:14.240
So when you're presented with facts that challenge
the world views of the groups that you associated

00:10:14.240 --> 00:10:15.240
with.

00:10:15.240 --> 00:10:16.450
It feels like a personal attack.

00:10:16.450 --> 00:10:18.260
You have an emotional response.

00:10:18.260 --> 00:10:22.940
And our brains are totally set up to defend
themselves.

00:10:22.940 --> 00:10:28.670
And we defend ourselves by through rationalize,
rationalization, confirmation bias, what we

00:10:28.670 --> 00:10:30.150
call motivated reasoning.

00:10:30.150 --> 00:10:31.950
You have a conclusion in mind.

00:10:31.950 --> 00:10:33.950
There's no such thing as climate change.

00:10:33.950 --> 00:10:38.170
And then you seek out reasons to believe that
and you discount the reasons you're given

00:10:38.170 --> 00:10:41.610
to believe that there is climate change.

00:10:41.610 --> 00:10:42.610
Right.

00:10:42.610 --> 00:10:45.930
And once again, in our media environment,
you can always find what you're looking for

00:10:45.930 --> 00:10:49.540
when you're looking for reasons to rationalize
